     1	//! Port of `net.minecraft.world.entity.ai.behavior` (26.2 decompile).
     2	//!
     3	//! Two layers, mirroring vanilla:
     4	//!
     5	//! - [`Behavior`] is `BehaviorControl<E>` (`behavior/BehaviorControl.java`), the only thing the
     6	//!   brain runtime knows about. `DoNothing`, `GateBehavior`/`RunOne` and the declarative
     7	//!   one-shots implement it directly, exactly as they do in Java.
     8	//! - [`TimedBehavior`] + [`TimedBehaviorControl`] together are `Behavior<E>`
     9	//!   (`behavior/Behavior.java`), the stateful start/tick/stop base with a randomized duration.
    10	//!   Java gets this via inheritance with `final tryStart`/`tickOrStop`/`doStop`; Rust cannot
    11	//!   express "final method on a supertype", and a blanket `impl<T: TimedBehavior> Behavior for T`
    12	//!   would collide (E0119) with the direct impls above. Wrapping in a concrete generic struct
    13	//!   keeps the lifecycle in exactly one place without the coherence fight.
    14	//!
    15	//! The trait is deliberately **synchronous**. Everything a behavior in this stage touches --
    16	//! `Navigator::set_progress`, `LookControl`, atomics on `Entity` -- is sync, so there is no way
    17	//! to accidentally hold the memory mutex across an `.await`. If a future behavior genuinely
    18	//! needs async, give it its own trait rather than making this one async.
    19	
